-- title:
--   Every Specht module with at most four rows maps into the cyclic module of the alternating path vector
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $[k]=\{0,1,\dots,k-1\}$. A *word* of length $n$ over the alphabet $[d]$ is a map $a:[n]\to[d]$, and $W_{n,d}$ is the complex vector space of all functions from such words to $\mathbb{C}$; it is the coordinate model of $(\mathbb{C}^d)^{\otimes n}$, the indicator $\mathbf{1}_a$ of a word playing the role of $e_{a(0)}\otimes\cdots\otimes e_{a(n-1)}$. The symmetric group $S_n$ acts on $W_{n,d}$ by permuting positions, $(g\cdot x)(a)=x(a\circ g)$, so that $g\cdot\mathbf{1}_a=\mathbf{1}_{a\circ g^{-1}}$. For a vector $v$ of a representation, $\langle v\rangle$ denotes the cyclic subrepresentation spanned by the orbit $\{g\cdot v\}$.
--
--   A Young diagram $\mu$ is a finite set of cells $(i,j)\in\mathbb{N}^2$ (row $i$, column $j$, both counted from $0$) closed under moving up or to the left, and $\ell(\mu)$ is its number of rows. A tableau of shape $\mu$ on $n$ positions is a bijection $t:[n]\to\mu$. Its row word $r_t:[n]\to[\ell(\mu)]$ sends $i$ to the row of $t(i)$; its column group $C_t\le S_n$ consists of the permutations $g$ such that $t(g(i))$ lies in the same column as $t(i)$ for every $i$; its polytabloid is $e_t=\sum_{g\in C_t}\operatorname{sgn}(g)\,g\cdot\mathbf{1}_{r_t}\in W_{n,\ell(\mu)}$; and its Specht module is the cyclic subrepresentation $S^t=\langle e_t\rangle\subseteq W_{n,\ell(\mu)}$.
--
--   Identify the alphabet $[4]$ with pairs $(x,y)\in\{0,1\}^2$, and let $J=\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\-1&0\end{pmatrix}$ with rows and columns indexed by $\{0,1\}$. For $r\in\mathbb{N}$ the *alternating path vector* $\Xi_r\in W_{2r+1,4}$ is defined on words $w$ with $w(q)=(x_q,y_q)$ by
--   $$\Xi_r(w)=[x_0=0]\,[y_{2r}=0]\prod_{k=0}^{r-1}J_{y_{2k}\,y_{2k+1}}\,J_{x_{2k+1}\,x_{2k+2}},$$
--   where $[\,\cdot\,]$ is $1$ if the condition holds and $0$ otherwise. Thus the first coordinates are antisymmetrised on the consecutive pairs of positions $\{1,2\},\{3,4\},\dots,\{2r-1,2r\}$ with the singleton $\{0\}$ carrying the letter $0$, and the second coordinates on the pairs $\{0,1\},\{2,3\},\dots,\{2r-2,2r-1\}$ with the singleton $\{2r\}$ carrying the letter $0$.
--
--   **Theorem.** Let $r\ge2$, let $\mu$ be a Young diagram with at most four rows, $\ell(\mu)\le4$, and let $t$ be a tableau of shape $\mu$ on $2r+1$ positions (so $|\mu|=2r+1$). Then there is a nonzero $S_{2r+1}$-equivariant linear map
--   $$f:S^t\longrightarrow\langle\Xi_r\rangle\subseteq W_{2r+1,4}.$$
--
--   Since Specht modules are irreducible, this says that every irreducible representation of $S_{2r+1}$ whose Young diagram has at most four rows occurs in the cyclic module generated by the alternating path vector. Up to sign and a relabelling of positions, $\Xi_r$ is the restriction to the letters $\{0,1\}$ of the band vector carried by the two outermost antidiagonals of the staircase $\delta_{r+1}$, so this is the base case for the band layers in the inductive analysis of the staircase tensor square.
--
--   **Formalization Note** $\Xi_r$ is `Path.bandWord r`, letters are `Fin 4` decoded into pairs by `Path.parts`, and the target is the subrepresentation `cyclic (wordRep (2*r+1) 4) (bandWord r)` with its restricted action. The hypothesis $r\ge2$ appears in the statement as `_hr`.
